WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.207
<v Examiner>Exam case 9.

2
00:00:02.207 --> 00:00:04.333
<v Examiner>Neurotransmitters and the brain.

3
00:00:04.783 --> 00:00:07.285
<v Examiner>Mr Paraone has been admitted to hospital after a fall,

4
00:00:07.285 --> 00:00:09.464
<v Examiner>and reports problems with mobility and tremors.

5
00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:11.735
<v Examiner>They're later diagnosed with Parkinson's disease:

6
00:00:11.735 --> 00:00:15.443
<v Examiner>a brain disease where the cells that normally make dopamine, a neurotransmitter,

7
00:00:15.443 --> 00:00:16.045
<v Examiner>start to die.

8
00:00:16.045 --> 00:00:17.250
<v Examiner>One treatment is Levodopa,

9
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.329
<v Examiner>which travels through the blood and into the brain to boost the brain's dopamine levels,

10
00:00:21.329 --> 00:00:24.388
<v Examiner>so the neurons can keep communicating properly via their synapses.

11
00:00:24.838 --> 00:00:25.511
Two tracks.

12
00:00:25.511 --> 00:00:27.163
First, how a synapse works.

13
00:00:27.613 --> 00:00:28.225
<v Examiner>Two marks.

14
00:00:28.225 --> 00:00:29.878
<v Examiner>What is a neurotransmitter?

15
00:00:29.878 --> 00:00:33.672
<v Examiner>Where is dopamine normally made in the central nervous system?

16
00:00:34.122 --> 00:00:39.342
A neurotransmitter is a chemical released at the presynaptic endings of a chemical

17
00:00:39.342 --> 00:00:44.116
synapse, which then acts on the receptor region of the postsynaptic neuron.

18
00:00:44.116 --> 00:00:47.808
Dopamine is made in the substantia nigra, in the midbrain,

19
00:00:47.808 --> 00:00:49.845
which is part of the brain stem.

20
00:00:50.295 --> 00:00:50.969
<v Examiner>Two marks.

21
00:00:50.969 --> 00:00:54.881
<v Examiner>Explain the role of calcium ions in synaptic transmission.

22
00:00:55.331 --> 00:00:59.028
When the action potential, the electrical nerve impulse,

23
00:00:59.028 --> 00:01:03.980
reaches the axon terminal of the presynaptic neuron, calcium channels open,

24
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.752
and calcium enters the presynaptic neuron.

25
00:01:06.752 --> 00:01:10.251
The calcium makes the neurotransmitter vesicles open,

26
00:01:10.251 --> 00:01:14.873
releasing the neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft, by exocytosis.

27
00:01:16.323 --> 00:01:16.975
<v Examiner>Six marks.

28
00:01:16.975 --> 00:01:22.843
<v Examiner>Explain the process of synaptic transmission that allows neurotransmitters to be released.

29
00:01:22.843 --> 00:01:24.408
<v Examiner>Use the diagram to help.

30
00:01:24.858 --> 00:01:26.360
Six marks, six steps.

31
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.938
The hook: arrive, calcium, release, cross, bind, respond, clear.

32
00:01:31.388 --> 00:01:31.667
One.

33
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:36.760
The action potential reaches the axon terminal of the presynaptic neuron,

34
00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.900
and calcium channels open, so calcium enters.

35
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:40.613
Two.

36
00:01:40.613 --> 00:01:46.129
The calcium makes the vesicles release the neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft,

37
00:01:46.129 --> 00:01:47.049
by exocytosis.

38
00:01:47.499 --> 00:01:47.846
Three.

39
00:01:47.846 --> 00:01:51.083
The neurotransmitter diffuses across the synaptic cleft.

40
00:01:51.533 --> 00:01:51.859
Four.

41
00:01:51.859 --> 00:01:55.053
It binds to receptors on the postsynaptic neuron.

42
00:01:55.503 --> 00:01:55.849
Five.

43
00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:59.239
The postsynaptic membrane's permeability changes,

44
00:01:59.239 --> 00:02:02.905
causing either an excitatory or an inhibitory effect.

45
00:02:03.355 --> 00:02:03.625
Six.

46
00:02:03.625 --> 00:02:08.689
The neurotransmitter is removed, by re-uptake, or by enzymatic destruction.

47
00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:10.727
<v Examiner>Two marks.

48
00:02:10.727 --> 00:02:14.021
<v Examiner>When neurotransmitters bind to a receptor, what happens?

49
00:02:14.471 --> 00:02:17.893
The postsynaptic membrane's permeability changes,

50
00:02:17.893 --> 00:02:21.594
causing either an excitatory or an inhibitory effect.

51
00:02:21.594 --> 00:02:25.714
The postsynaptic neuron is either stimulated, or inhibited.

52
00:02:26.164 --> 00:02:26.686
<v Examiner>One mark.

53
00:02:26.686 --> 00:02:31.497
<v Examiner>After the neurotransmitter has been released, what happens to that chemical signal?

54
00:02:31.947 --> 00:02:32.821
It's removed.

55
00:02:32.821 --> 00:02:38.398
Either by re-uptake, back into the presynaptic neuron, or by enzymatic destruction.

56
00:02:38.398 --> 00:02:40.481
Take it back, or break it down.

57
00:02:40.931 --> 00:02:41.512
<v Examiner>Once more.

58
00:02:41.512 --> 00:02:42.211
<v Examiner>The synapse.

59
00:02:44.661 --> 00:02:50.043
A neurotransmitter is a chemical released at the presynaptic endings of a chemical

60
00:02:50.043 --> 00:02:54.245
synapse, which acts on the receptors of the postsynaptic neuron.

61
00:02:54.245 --> 00:02:57.986
Dopamine is made in the substantia nigra of the midbrain.

62
00:02:57.986 --> 00:03:02.581
The action potential reaches the axon terminal, calcium channels open,

63
00:03:02.581 --> 00:03:08.161
and calcium makes the vesicles release neurotransmitter into the cleft by exocytosis.

64
00:03:08.161 --> 00:03:12.297
It diffuses across, binds receptors on the postsynaptic neuron,

65
00:03:12.297 --> 00:03:16.695
and changes the membrane's permeability: excitatory, or inhibitory.

66
00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:20.437
Then it's cleared, by re-uptake or enzymatic destruction.
